it's been called the worst flooding to  hit india's northeastern assam state the  rain may have stopped now but the flood  waters are still here inundating large  areas of the region many have lost  everything unsure of how or where they  will rebuild their lives  one month has passed since we started  living here and our fields and crops  have submerged in water our house is  inundated now this road has become our  home  more than 200 people have died and  millions have been displaced by the  severe floods that swept across india's  northeast region and neighboring  bangladesh medical teams are still  trying to reach affected areas they say  many are now at risk of contracting  water-borne diseases regarding  post-flood management there are many  issues like  acute gastroenteritis  then fever  many cases has been admitted at various  hospitals  every year heavy monsoon rains cause  rivers to flood but this year was worse  than ever as a some and neighboring  megalia state received more rain than  average the eroding riverbanks failed to  hold the excess water resulting in  floods and landslides like this one in  manipur state  and worsening the situation in  bangladesh which shares common rivers  with india officials say dredging is the  only way to prevent floods like these  last week bangladesh's prime minister  proposed a joint river dredging project  with india to help the region  but for those who have already lost  their loved ones and their livelihoods a  solution can't come soon enough  kara legge al jazeera
 

